TY  - JOUR
AU  - Giacomo Pallante
TI  - Youth entrepreneurship and credit access in sub-Saharan Africa: evidence from Uganda’s youth venture capital fund
JO  - Applied Economics Letters
PY  - 2025
AB  - Among the barriers hindering African development, improving access to credit for businesses remains a significant challenge, particularly for young borrowers seeking favourable loan conditions. This paper analyzes the impact of the Youth Venture Capital Fund (YVCF) program, expanded in Uganda in 2013 to improve access to credit and foster youth entrepreneurship within the eligible cohort of individuals aged 18–35. The analysis uses data from LSMA-ISA surveys of the World Bank, a nationally representative dataset spanning eight waves from 2009 to 2016. Adopting a difference-in-differences approach, we estimate the intention-to-treat effect on the eligible population. Results show a significant increase in the likelihood of starting a new business, a modest rise in monthly per capita expenditure, and a positive effect on the survival rate of newly established enterprises. However, the benefits are not uniformly persistent: the effects on business creation and expenditure diminish within one to two years, whereas the survival rate of new enterprises remains positively impacted for at least four years from the policy introduction. Heterogeneous effects are observed, with female-headed enterprises experiencing the largest and most enduring benefits. Conversely, the youngest cohort in the targeted population does not significantly benefit from the program.
